issues.problematic_code_snippet:
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Endpoints;
using Microsoft.AspNetCore.DataProtection;
using Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions;

namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Server.Circuits
{
    public class ServerComponentDeserializerTest
    {

var parameters = deserializedDescriptor.Parameters.ToDictionary();
Assert.Single(parameters);
Assert.Contains("Value", parameters.Keys);
Assert.Equal(42, Convert.ToInt64(parameters["Value"]!, CultureInfo.InvariantCulture));

var secondDescriptor = descriptors[1];
Assert.Equal(typeof(GenericTestComponent<string>).FullName, secondDescriptor.ComponentType.FullName);
Assert.Equal(0, secondDescriptor.Sequence);

var preamble = match.Groups["preamble"].Value;
var preambleMarker = JsonSerializer.Deserialize<ComponentMarker>(preamble, ServerComponentSerializationSettings.JsonSerializationOptions);
Assert.NotNull(preambleMarker.PrerenderId);
Assert.Equal("webassembly", preambleMarker.Type);
Assert.Equal(typeof(GenericComponent<string>).Assembly.GetName().Name, preambleMarker.Assembly);
Assert.Equal(typeof(GenericComponent<string>).FullName, preambleMarker.TypeName);

findings.body:
The test prerenders `GenericComponent<int>`, but its type assertions use `GenericComponent<string>`:

::diff{file="src/Components/Endpoints/test/EndpointHtmlRendererTest.cs" lines="833-838"}

`WebAssemblyComponentSerializer.SerializeInvocation` writes `type.FullName` into the marker. So `preambleMarker.TypeName` will be ``...GenericComponent`1[[System.Int32, System.Private.CoreLib, ...]]``, while the expected value is ``...[[System.String, ...]]``. The `Assembly` assertion only passes by accident, because both closed types live in the same assembly.

::callout{type="warning"}
This test will fail in CI. This is exactly the generic-argument check the PR is meant to cover, so it's the assertion that most needs to be right.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/Endpoints/test/EndpointHtmlRendererTest.cs" lines="837-838"}
        Assert.Equal(typeof(GenericComponent<int>).Assembly.GetName().Name, preambleMarker.Assembly);
        Assert.Equal(typeof(GenericComponent<int>).FullName, preambleMarker.TypeName);

You could also assert on the parameter definitions and values in the marker, like `CanPrerender_ComponentWithParameters_ClientMode` does. That would show `Value` serializes as `System.Int32`.

`CreateMarkers` gives one invocation's markers consecutive sequence numbers (0, 1, ...). `ServerComponentDeserializer` also rejects any descriptor whose sequence isn't `lastSequence + 1` (see `ServerComponentDeserializer.cs:114-123`). So `descriptors[1].Sequence` must be 1:

::diff{file="src/Components/Server/test/Circuits/ServerComponentDeserializerTest.cs" lines="143-145"}

The existing `CanParseMultipleMarkers` test a few lines below asserts `Assert.Equal(1, secondDescriptor.Sequence)`. This new test will fail.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/Server/test/Circuits/ServerComponentDeserializerTest.cs" lines="145-145"}
        Assert.Equal(1, secondDescriptor.Sequence);

The PR changes the file's `namespace ...;` declaration into a `namespace ... { public class ... {` block. The class body keeps its original indentation, and the file now ends with two stacked `}` braces:

::diff{file="src/Components/Server/test/Circuits/ServerComponentDeserializerTest.cs" lines="10-16"}

None of the new tests need this change. It goes against the repo-wide file-scoped namespace convention (`.editorconfig` / IDE0161) and leaves the whole class indented wrongly relative to its braces, so `dotnet format` will flag or rewrite it. It also adds diff noise to a file that should only be gaining a few test methods.

::suggestion{file="src/Components/Server/test/Circuits/ServerComponentDeserializerTest.cs" lines="10-13"}
namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Server.Circuits;

public class ServerComponentDeserializerTest
{

Also remove the extra closing `}` at the end of the file.

All the new tests close the generic over `int` or `string`, both from `System.Private.CoreLib`. The issue's main scenario is a wrapper like `ProtectedWidget<TComponent>`, where the type argument is a user type from an app or Razor class library assembly. `GetRootType` resolves the name with the root component's assembly. A type argument from a third assembly must be found through the assembly-qualified inner name, and nothing here tests that.

::diff{file="src/Components/Server/test/Circuits/ServerComponentDeserializerTest.cs" lines="79-91"}

If the PR is going to close dotnet/aspnetcore#27579 with tests alone, add one case with a non-BCL type argument, e.g. `GenericTestComponent<TestComponent>` or a type from another test assembly. That would back the "already works" claim more strongly.
